@page "/HowItWorks/partner-transfers"
@using InvestmentTaxCalculator.Components

<HowItWorksGuideLayout PageAddress="HowItWorks/partner-transfers">
    <ChildContent>
        <div class="section">
            <p>
                Transfers between spouses or civil partners who live together are treated as giving neither a gain nor a loss
                (TCGA 1992 s58). The shares move with their original cost. You record them on the
                <a href="corporate-actions" class="link-styled">Corporate Actions and Events</a> page.
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Gift to partner:</strong> you enter the share, date and number of shares. They are removed from your
                    Section 104 pool at the pool's average cost, with no gain or loss. The cost transferred, which your partner needs
                    for their own records, is shown in the pool's history on the
                    <a href="Section104DataPage" class="link-styled">Section 104 Data</a> page. You cannot give more shares than the pool holds on that
                    date.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Received from partner:</strong> you enter the number of shares and the cost your partner had for
                    them. They are added to your pool with that cost.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Transfers only change the pool. They are not disposals or acquisitions for the same-day or 30-day rules.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="card mb-4">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h2 class="h5 card-title">Worked example</h2>
                <p class="mb-0">
                    Your pool holds 300 shares costing £3,600. You give 100 to your spouse. They leave the pool with
                    £3,600 × 100/300 = <strong>£1,200</strong> of cost, and your pool keeps 200 shares costing £2,400. Your
                    spouse records 100 shares received with a cost of £1,200.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </ChildContent>
    <FurtherReading>
        <li><a href="https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1992/12/section/58" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">TCGA 1992 s58</a>: spouses and civil partners</li>
        <li><a href="HowItWorks/section-104-pool" class="link-styled">Section 104 pool</a></li>
    </FurtherReading>
</HowItWorksGuideLayout>
